---
import { EXTENSION_META } from '@data/extension-meta';
import LegalLayout from '@layouts/legal-layout.astro';

const issuesUrl = `${EXTENSION_META.repositoryUrl}/issues`;
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — HeaderTweaker"
  description="HeaderTweaker collects no personal data. Your header rules and settings stay in your browser's local storage and are never sent anywhere."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  lastUpdated={new Date('2026-11-07')}
>
  <p>
    This policy explains how the {EXTENSION_META.name} browser extension (for Firefox and Chrome)
    and this website, headertweaker.com, handle your data. The short version:&nbsp;
    <strong>HeaderTweaker does not collect, transmit, sell or share any personal data.</strong>
    &nbsp;Everything you configure stays in your own browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    {EXTENSION_META.name}
    is a free, open-source project maintained by the developer(s) of the HeaderTweaker repository
    and released under the MIT License. The complete source code is public on&nbsp;
    <a href={EXTENSION_META.repositoryUrl}>GitHub</a>, so every statement in this policy can be
    verified against the code.
  </p>

  <h2>Data the extension stores</h2>
  <p>
    To work, the extension keeps the following on your device, using the browser's local storage API
    (<code>storage.local</code>):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your header rules</strong>&nbsp;
      — each rule's name, value, optional label, the URLs it is scoped to, and whether it is
      enabled.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your settings</strong>&nbsp;
      — whether the extension is switched on, whether labels are shown, and the column widths of the
      header list.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This data never leaves your browser through HeaderTweaker. It is not synced to an account, not
    uploaded to a server, and not accessible to the developer. Removing the extension removes this
    data from your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    Header values often contain sensitive information, such as API keys or authentication tokens.
    The browser stores them on your device without additional encryption, so treat your browser
    profile, and any exported files (see below), with the same care as the credentials themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>How web requests are handled</h2>
  <p>
    HeaderTweaker's only job is to add or replace headers on outgoing HTTP requests that match your
    rules. It never reads, logs, stores or transmits the contents of your requests or responses.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Chrome</strong>&nbsp;
      uses the <code>declarativeNetRequestWithHostAccess</code> API. The extension hands your rules
      to the browser, and Chrome applies them itself — the extension never sees the requests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Firefox</strong>&nbsp;
      uses the <code>webRequest</code> API. The extension sees an outgoing request's URL and headers
      just long enough to apply your rules, in memory, before the request is sent. Nothing is
      recorded.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The headers you configure are, by design, sent to the websites your rules target. What those
    websites do with them is governed by their own privacy policies.
  </p>

  <h2>Current tab address</h2>
  <p>
    The extension looks at the address of the tab you're viewing to show how many of your rules
    apply to it (the toolbar badge) and to pre-fill the URL when you scope a rule to the current
    site. This is done locally, in memory, and the address is only saved if you add it to a rule
    yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Import, export and clipboard</h2>
  <p>
    Exporting saves your header rules as a JSON file on your device; importing reads a JSON file you
    select. Clicking a header value copies it to your clipboard. All of these happen only when you
    ask for them, and the files and copied text stay under your control. HeaderTweaker does not send
    them anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics, tracking, telemetry or crash reporting.</li>
    <li>No accounts, sign-ins or cookies.</li>
    <li>No advertising, and no selling or transferring of data to third parties.</li>
    <li>No remote code — only the code that ships in the installed package runs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Chrome Web Store User Data Policy</h2>
  <p>
    HeaderTweaker's use of information received from browser APIs adheres to the&nbsp;
    <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/webstore/program-policies/user-data-faq"
      >Chrome Web Store User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements. Data is used solely to provide the extension's single
    purpose of modifying outgoing HTTP request headers, and is never transferred, sold, used for
    advertising, or used to determine creditworthiness or for lending purposes.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    headertweaker.com is a static website. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics or tracking
    scripts, and serves its fonts itself rather than loading them from a third party. Like any web
    server, the hosting provider may briefly process technical data such as your IP address and
    browser type to deliver pages and protect the site from abuse; this data is not used to identify
    or profile you.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    HeaderTweaker is a developer tool and is not directed at children. Because it collects no
    personal data, it collects none from children either.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If HeaderTweaker's data handling changes, this page will be updated and the "Last updated" date
    above will change. The history of this page is public in the&nbsp;
    <a href={EXTENSION_META.repositoryUrl}>source repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about this policy? Open an issue on <a href={issuesUrl}>GitHub</a>.</p>
</LegalLayout>
